# Composite

Treat leaves and groups the same: `getPrice()`, `getCount()`, `display()` work on a product **and** a category of products.

## Anatomy

- Component interface.
- Leaf (no children).
- Composite (list of components; delegates recursively).
- A service **builds** the tree; it is a provider. Nodes are plain classes.

```ts
export interface CatalogComponent {
  getName(): string;
  getPrice(): number;
  getCount(): number;
}

export class ProductItem implements CatalogComponent {
  constructor(
    private readonly name: string,
    private readonly price: number,
  ) {}

  getName() {
    return this.name;
  }
  getPrice() {
    return this.price;
  }
  getCount() {
    return 1;
  }
}

export class Category implements CatalogComponent {
  private readonly children: CatalogComponent[] = [];

  add(child: CatalogComponent) {
    this.children.push(child);
  }

  getPrice() {
    return this.children.reduce((sum, child) => sum + child.getPrice(), 0);
  }

  getCount() {
    return this.children.reduce((count, child) => count + child.getCount(), 0);
  }
}
```

## Nest mapping

Do not register `ProductItem` / `Category` as providers. A `CatalogService.buildCatalog()` (or a use-case that loads rows then assembles the tree) is enough.

This repo’s spaces/files are **flat lists** with `sortOrder`, not nested catalogs. Do not introduce Composite for `SpaceFile[]`.

## When to use

- Real part–whole trees (menus, nested categories, folder trees).
- The same operation should recurse without `if (isFolder)`.

## When not to use

- Flat arrays — `map` / `reduce`.
- Leaves and groups need different APIs and pretending they are the same hides bugs.
- You need the type system to forbid `add` on leaves.

Related: `nest-decorator.mdc`, `nest-iterator.mdc`, `nest-visitor.mdc`.
